" Mouse selection goes to the X PRIMARY selection -- but through the editor,
" not around it.  The obvious reading is that select-to-PRIMARY belongs to the
" terminal, so the editor should set mouse= and get out of the way.  That works,
" and it leaves the problem it was supposed to solve: a terminal drag selects
" *screen columns*, so it drags in the 'number' gutter, the signcolumn, and --
" new in the nvim era -- the diagnostic virtual text hanging off the line end.
" The workaround for that is :set nonu, copy, :set nu, forever.
"
" Letting nvim own the mouse removes the problem instead of managing it: nvim
" can only select buffer text, so no gutter exists to be caught.  <LeftRelease>
" then yanks the visual selection into "* -- PRIMARY, per :h primary-selection,
" so middle-click paste works and CLIPBOARD ("+, ctrl-c/ctrl-v) is never
" touched, which is the point.
"
" Measured with injected SGR mouse events, 'number' on, dragging screen columns
" 6..13 of "  1 ABCDEFGHIJ...": the register held exactly BCDEFGHI.  The xclip
" the provider spawns reparents to PID 1, so the selection outlives nvim -- :h
" clipboard-x11 warns otherwise, and on this implementation it does not hold.
"
" The escape hatch, when a terminal-native selection really is wanted (output
" from :! , another tmux pane): urxvt overrides mouse reporting while Shift or
" Meta is held -- its own man page, MOUSE REPORTING.  tmux is set mouse off
" here, so it is not a third layer in the way.
"
" nvim only, and not by preference: Arch's vim is built -clipboard, so
" has('clipboard') is 0 there and "* is an ordinary register that never reaches
" X.  Its 'ttymouse' also defaults to xterm under screen-256color, the protocol
" that caps at column 223 and does not report drag or release.  Both would have
" to change -- the first means the gvim package -- so vim keeps mouse= and the
" terminal keeps selecting for it.
if has('nvim')
    set mouse=a
    vnoremap <silent> <LeftRelease> "*y
else
    set mouse=
endif

